Q: UNION vs UNION ALL in MySQL?

Both combine results of two or more SELECTs into one result set.
Difference = **duplicates** (and therefore speed).

────────────────────────────────────────
1) UNION
- Combines SELECT results
- **Removes duplicate rows** (like DISTINCT on the final set)
- Extra work → often **slower** on large data

SELECT first_name FROM employees
UNION
SELECT first_name FROM customers;

→ unique first_names only (if same name in both tables → once)

────────────────────────────────────────
2) UNION ALL
- Combines SELECT results
- **Keeps all rows**, including duplicates
- No dedupe step → usually **faster**

SELECT first_name FROM employees
UNION ALL
SELECT first_name FROM customers;

→ names from both; overlap appears twice

────────────────────────────────────────
Rules (both)

1) Same number of columns in each SELECT
2) Compatible data types (by position)
3) Column names in result usually come from the **first** SELECT
4) ORDER BY applies to the final combined result (place at the end)

SELECT a FROM t1
UNION ALL
SELECT b FROM t2
ORDER BY a;

────────────────────────────────────────
When to use which

| Need                         | Use        |
| ---------------------------- | ---------- |
| Unique combined rows         | UNION      |
| Keep duplicates / sure no overlap | UNION ALL |
| Best performance by default  | UNION ALL (if duplicates OK) |

────────────────────────────────────────
Summary / interview one-liner

“UNION merges and removes duplicates; UNION ALL merges and keeps duplicates, so it’s usually faster. Prefer UNION ALL unless you truly need distinct rows.”
